{"new_lineages":[{"id":"L-equivalence-undecidable","scope":"language","statement":"Extensional program equivalence is undecidable in general, so statistical evidence only gives a limited probabilistic guarantee, not a formal proof."},{"id":"L-external-correctness-uncontrollable","scope":"language","statement":"External(T) is an FFI problem under dependent types whose promised correctness depends on the external code's real semantics, outside Luthier's control."},{"id":"L-forgetting-semantic-not-syntactic","scope":"language","statement":"A forgetful functor is a semantic notion, not a syntactic operation on type terms, and its claimed commutativity is assumed rather than proven."},{"id":"L-ai-acritical-amplifier","scope":"method","statement":"The Luthier AI acted as an acritical amplifier, celebrating every idea without flagging contradictions, undecidability, or the real state of the art."},{"id":"L-unification-unfeasible-now","scope":"language","statement":"Unifying HoTT, Bayesian inference and reflective meta-programming into one operative language is, at the present state of the art, infeasible."},{"id":"L-three-primitives-types-terms-paths","scope":"language","statement":"The paper-and-pencil core of Luthier reduces to three logical primitives: Types, Terms and Paths."},{"id":"L-delegation-easy","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier should make delegation easy."},{"id":"L-modelica-spec-without-implementation","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier draws on Modelica, a specification of a program without its implementation, separating what is wanted from how it is implemented."},{"id":"L-description-first","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier does execute, but its emphasis and first stage is describing the problem, the functions and their conditions, not execution; it could even be a dead language that only describes."},{"id":"L-execution-is-equivalence-verification","scope":"language","statement":"Execution in Luthier is the verification of equivalences, since mathematics does not execute but only verifies equivalences."},{"id":"L-levels-of-equivalence","scope":"language","statement":"A fundamental idea of Luthier is to express the different levels of equivalence (same information, isomorphic, exactly equal, same memory location), each depending on a different equivalence functor."},{"id":"L-equivalence-separates-essence-decoration","scope":"language","statement":"Function equivalences separate what is essential (the equivalent) from decoration (efficiency, cost), which is what is not equivalent."},{"id":"L-core-few-native-primitives","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier should have a core in which all its operations execute, with only a few primitives implemented in C or similar so it runs."},{"id":"L-facilities-not-direct-translation","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier does not translate to other languages directly but provides the facilities so functions and equivalences can be mapped and compiled to C, C++ or Java and extended by others."},{"id":"L-category-style-primitives","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier's basic elements are defined category-theory style, from arrows, as functors are defined from arrows."},{"id":"L-forgetting-in-hott","scope":"language","statement":"Forgetting is already present in HoTT, in the sense that a type forgets parts of the type."},{"id":"L-hott-probabilities-constructible","scope":"language","statement":"HoTT does not work on probabilities natively, but probabilities could be built within it."},{"id":"L-minimalism-in-foundations","scope":"language","statement":"There is no contradiction between minimalism and maximalism: the minimalism is in the founding components, from which a huge edifice of complex concepts is built."},{"id":"L-no-execution-until-defined","scope":"language","statement":"Code is not executed until it is fully defined, so undefined dead code can sit there without bothering anyone."},{"id":"L-program-as-function-or-interactive","scope":"language","statement":"An open design question is whether a Luthier program is a pure function (input to output) or has an interaction function; the function model comes first for simplicity, extendable later."},{"id":"L-code-many-operations","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier code is not merely compiled and run; many operations can be applied to it: verify, compile, run and others."},{"id":"L-hot-values-path-universe","scope":"language","statement":"Hot Values give a larger universe of possible paths, an expansion of the exploration space over ordinary values."},{"id":"L-particle-filtering-paths","scope":"language","statement":"To manage the exponential explosion of the enlarged path universe, use particle filtering: run several programs in parallel with stochastic programming exploring different paths, replicating a hot basin."},{"id":"L-mean-is-forgetful-functor","scope":"language","statement":"A mean is a kind of forgetful functor, a mechanism that summarizes information by discarding detail."},{"id":"L-express-then-choose-method","scope":"method","statement":"A design method: first express something well in the language, which lets you express both hits and errors, and then choose the hits."},{"id":"L-pencil-and-database-two-layers","scope":"language","statement":"A pencil-and-paper language and one needing a database of empirical runs are not opposite philosophies but two layers or moments: you express the ideas with pencil and paper and then go to execution and the database."},{"id":"L-quantifiers-first-class","scope":"language","statement":"The logical quantifiers 'for all' and 'exists' must be first-class citizens of Luthier."},{"id":"L-limits-in-language","scope":"language","statement":"Whether limits should be writable in the language; since limits are written with 'for all' and 'exists', the quantifiers already cover them."},{"id":"L-first-class-citizens-open","scope":"language","statement":"Which elements are first-class citizens in Luthier remains to be reviewed."},{"id":"L-von-neumann-epigraph","scope":"other","statement":"Luthier's epigraph is Von Neumann's reply: define exactly what thinking is and he'll build a machine that does it; Luthier is the project of making machines do whatever you can define well."},{"id":"L-language-for-defining-ideas","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier is a language for defining things, where ideas are first-class citizens, like natural languages such as Spanish or English."},{"id":"L-concrete-case-reveals-design","scope":"method","statement":"Take a concrete implementation need (organizing the Gaussian-Newton algorithm class) and use it to ask what the underlying Luthier design problem is."},{"id":"L-class-brings-structure-and-records","scope":"language","statement":"Apply the Vector Space logic to a class: generate a class that already carries its structure and accessible records, rather than a bare structure."},{"id":"L-generic-iteration-status-class","scope":"language","statement":"Iteration and status are concepts more general than their version in a particular algorithm, so there can be a generic status or iteration class, the instance knowing its algorithm from the context it sits inside."},{"id":"L-independent-vs-bound-concepts","scope":"language","statement":"Some concepts can live independently of their container (a mean, separable from the variance) while others are intrinsically bound (an iteration is always the iteration of an algorithm)."},{"id":"L-iteration-carries-algorithm-id","scope":"language","statement":"Because an iteration is bound to an algorithm, it should carry the identifier of the algorithm that creates it, itself structured (a method that is a creation of an MLE or MAP, which may use Gaussian-Newton)."},{"id":"L-record-as-relational-database","scope":"language","statement":"What a record is can be stored as a relational database: fields with elements and relations, a record being a subset of the cartesian product with duplicates removed."},{"id":"L-forget-as-projection","scope":"language","statement":"A first form of the forget function: get_iteration drops the whole context and leaves a bare iteration; forgetting is a projection onto an axis, a reduction of dimensionality."},{"id":"L-rejoin-projections-recover","scope":"language","statement":"The projections can be rejoined to recover the original object; the interesting case is disjoint or complementary (orthogonal) projections, reconstructible by pullback."},{"id":"L-database-schema-is-category","scope":"language","statement":"A database schema is a category (tables objects, foreign keys morphisms) and the loaded data is a functor."},{"id":"L-database-topology","scope":"language","statement":"The latent-structure questions of databases are problems of discrete topology."},{"id":"L-topology-reduces-to-integers","scope":"other","statement":"Topology reduces geometry to the integers."},{"id":"L-only-integers-exist","scope":"other","statement":"Only the integers exist; the reals are a cartesian product of integers projected by powers of two."},{"id":"L-iteration-as-successor-construction","scope":"language","statement":"Iteration is defined by how it is built: an algorithm in a state has the step applied and the iteration increments (the successor), a dimension governed by the Peano naturals, and Luthier should write that easily."},{"id":"L-status-as-enumerated-states","scope":"language","statement":"A status cannot be a string but a number of possible algorithm states (converged, not converged, ...), each defined by its invariants."},{"id":"L-newton-raphson-first-application","scope":"method","statement":"Write Newton-Raphson with all its invariants typed as Luthier's initial application, possibly publishable."},{"id":"L-publish-arxiv","scope":"method","statement":"Publish it the way physicists do, on arXiv, and let it spread."},{"id":"L-grammar-and-interpreter","scope":"method","statement":"Specify the language with a grammar and implement it in weeks to have a DSL that applies Luthier; an interpreter is already running for some things."},{"id":"L-successor-beyond-naturals","scope":"language","statement":"The successor function could apply to sets other than the naturals (the successor of an algorithm, the successor in the presidency); open whether it only makes sense under an isomorphism with the naturals."},{"id":"L-topos-talk-validates","scope":"other","statement":"The Topos Institute talk on higher-dimensional syntax is the theoretical base of what is designed for Luthier, uniting HoTT, constructive logic and a commutative graph structure."}],
