{"new_lineages":[{"id":"L-forward-vs-history-info","scope":"language","statement":"A state engine distinguishes information that flows forward from information kept for history, as Luthier's context manager and memory."},{"id":"L-error-as-remembering","scope":"language","statement":"Error messages are the opposite of forgetting, a well-structured remembering of why one erred, a comonad dual to the forgetful functor."},{"id":"L-error-as-subgraph","scope":"language","statement":"In a graph-based program an error returns the failed sub-graph, with each node carrying an origin/trace consulted when validation fails."},{"id":"L-ai-needs-structure","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier's homoiconic, graph-and-dependent-type structure gives an AI the structural reasoning about the world that statistical LLMs lack."},{"id":"L-build-before-ai-falls","scope":"method","statement":"Luthier should be built now, before the highly qualified AI guidance might be restricted or all this falls."},{"id":"L-luthier-abstract-algebra","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier has to do with abstract algebra."},{"id":"L-platform-sharing-wins","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier is a platform where sharing your information makes you win, because the act of sharing raises the value of your own information."},{"id":"L-correlated-data-worth-more","scope":"other","statement":"Correlated data points are worth more than isolated ones, and detecting larger patterns needs ever more data."},{"id":"L-data-upload-reciprocity","scope":"language","statement":"The minimal reciprocity of uploading your datum: the database gains your datum and you learn where you stand relative to the rest."},{"id":"L-error-management-guarantees","scope":"language","statement":"The point of Luthier is that error management be better and results be guaranteed."},{"id":"L-value-knows-operation","scope":"language","statement":"A value knows the concrete operation that produced it: if it is the sum, it is the sum and not the generic magma operation."},{"id":"L-views-of-operations","scope":"language","statement":"From a series of operations one can take different views or levels: the magma, the result, the execution, the code."},{"id":"L-luthierize-foreign-code","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier annotates foreign code (luthierizes it), e.g. matrix-inversion libraries, starting from the definition of what the function should do and the property it must satisfy."},{"id":"L-properties-hold-within-error","scope":"language","statement":"Declared properties (like an inverse recovering the value) hold only within a certain error, since inverse functions are not perfect."},{"id":"L-value-as-interval-sample","scope":"language","statement":"A value is really an interval of values or a sample of values (as a Markov chain represented by a sample)."},{"id":"L-precision-depends-on-history","scope":"language","statement":"The recoverable precision of a value depends on how much of its operational history is kept; forgetting the operation turns an exact value into an interval."},{"id":"L-code-follows-laws-not-floats","scope":"language","statement":"Floating-point numbers do not obey the algebraic laws of numbers, but the code is assumed to, so it can be reorganized for a more efficient or more perfect execution."},{"id":"L-language-ai-uses","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier should be a language used by the AI itself, not only by a human."},{"id":"L-self-hack-risk","scope":"language","statement":"A risk to resolve is that the language or system auto-hacks itself."},{"id":"L-execution-as-forgetting","scope":"language","statement":"Execution is a forgetful function: code generates code, with two levels, one that keeps the generated code and one that forgets it."},{"id":"L-objective-unclear","scope":"method","statement":"What Luthier is actually for is still open; so far it is an intellectual adventure whose objective is yet to be found."},{"id":"L-pyluthier-type-as-decoration","scope":"language","statement":"In a Python implementation the type is not a real host type but floats as an attribute, represented by fields and decorations; the easy but imperfect route."},{"id":"L-start-with-easiest-luthier","scope":"method","statement":"Start with the easiest route, a kind of Luthier, rather than the harder C++ one, before committing to the final form."},{"id":"L-build-own-language-directly","scope":"language","statement":"An alternative to implementing Luthier inside a host language is to build directly a language of one's own."},{"id":"L-recover-meaning-via-luthier","scope":"method","statement":"Since the AI now writes all the code and he no longer sees or feels it, Luthier is the way to recover contact with meaning and have things guaranteed without looking at the code."},{"id":"L-spec-after-cycle","scope":"method","statement":"The specification is the first document that makes decisions (excluding what the audios still let coexist), written on paper after the audios-ideas-consolidation-mentor cycle."},{"id":"L-infectability-lens","scope":"method","statement":"Consolidation uses infectability as its lens (the ideas that most change how you program are the ones that most structure the language), a choice specific to this project."},{"id":"L-mentor-friction","scope":"method","statement":"The mentor step takes the consolidation and produces friction on the design process without designing itself, and Luciano answers in new audios, closing the cycle."},{"id":"L-pipeline-method","scope":"method","statement":"The working method is the pipeline audios then ideas then consolidation then mentor."},{"id":"L-date-both-consolidations","scope":"method","statement":"Keep the earlier consolidation by renaming it and dating both versions, so the ecosystem's evolution can be compared."},{"id":"L-example-before-principle","scope":"method","statement":"No principle enters the specification without an example worked by hand in the candidate notation; the canonical cases test whether there is a language."},{"id":"L-pipeline-forgetting","scope":"method","statement":"The consolidation cost growing with the corpus is the forget-functor problem applied to the pipeline itself, to be handled by consolidating in batches or from a previous consolidate."},{"id":"L-grows-to-universe-risk","scope":"method","statement":"Luthier risks accumulating without advancing and diffusing until it encompasses the whole universe, becoming useless; it must stay operative and practical."},{"id":"L-two-growth-paths","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier can grow two ways: as a universal arena, or more simply as a lambda-calculus-like, more down-to-earth language."},{"id":"L-universal-yet-semantic","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier should be algebraically universal (able to write almost anything, like a universal Turing machine) while every element is semantically defined and human-understandable."},{"id":"L-python-spirit","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier imitates Python's spirit of letting human ideas be expressed easily (with the Monty Python / Luthier wordplay)."},{"id":"L-recursive-lisp-nicer","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier is imagined as a recursive language in the line of Lisp but nicer."},{"id":"L-code-mathematical-equivalence","scope":"language","statement":"A Luthier code has a clear mathematical equivalence."},{"id":"L-what-is-a-program","scope":"language","statement":"The basic question of Luthier: what is a Luthier program, a map or a graph?"},{"id":"L-program-composition-harmonic","scope":"language","statement":"The fundamental problem is how Luthier programs connect and expand one another so they compose and grow harmonically."},{"id":"L-compile-detects-incompatibility","scope":"language","statement":"Compiling checks whether code contains incompatibilities (errors); many programs must be non-compilable, unlike an algebraic calculation where any expression computes something."},{"id":"L-universal-adapter","scope":"language","statement":"Using a library requires it to follow patterns and satisfy propositions defined from Luthier, and an adapter fits each library to the use, so Luthier works as a universal adapter between libraries."},{"id":"L-store-record-function-version","scope":"language","statement":"Apply Luthier to the analysis store by recording each result's generating function and version and keeping a table of functions."},{"id":"L-representation-fields-vs-json","scope":"language","statement":"Whether a result is represented with fixed fields or relational JSON: the set of result types is open but each type is closed, a sum of types rather than a JSON blob."},{"id":"L-schema-from-type","scope":"language","statement":"The storage schema is generated from the function's declared type in the code rather than hand-written, and migration is derived."},{"id":"L-closed-vs-open-world","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier verifies a closed world at compile time; for open-world field data, invariants are required at creation of derived objects but observed data gets a validation with a verdict, never a rejection."},{"id":"L-derived-is-proof-term","scope":"language","statement":"A derived object (inputs, function, parameters, result) is a proof term whose type is the proposition that the result was obtained that way, so the database is a store of proofs."},{"id":"L-verify-by-sampling","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier verifies postconditions by sampling possible inputs, and postcondition verification and coverage calibration are the same computation."},{"id":"L-precondition-is-type","scope":"language","statement":"A precondition is guaranteed by the argument's type, verified when the reference is formed; predicates on values require refined types, and a function failing its postcondition yields a stored failed-attempt object."},{"id":"L-original-vs-derivative-types","scope":"language","statement":"There are original types generated from audios and questionnaires; all other types are derivative (results of functions) and therefore necessarily structured."},{"id":"L-function-must-be-deterministic","scope":"language","statement":"If identity is (inputs, function, version, parameters), the function must be deterministic: the seed and everything from the environment enter as explicit arguments, or the certificate lies."},{"id":"L-never-edit-derived","scope":"language","statement":"A derived object is never edited; corrections are new human assertions (originals), so labels, quality control and corrections all land in the same place."},{"id":"L-couple-with-sonolocalizacion","scope":"method","statement":"Luthier is a meta-project whose next development couples to sonolocalizacion, at the library stage (and macro IR); how to relate the two is still to be resolved."},{"id":"L-butler-of-knowledge","scope":"language","statement":"Luthier is used together with the AI as a kind of butler of knowledge."},{"id":"L-must-be-useful","scope":"method","statement":"Luthier must be useful and take root in something real, not stay purely theoretical, used at a rough level to hit reality and reveal its limits."},{"id":"L-usefulness-gap","scope":"method","statement":"Find the gap of usefulness, a concrete place from which Luthier can begin to develop while being useful."},{"id":"L-fundamental-elements-question","scope":"language","statement":"What are the most fundamental elements of a program: elementary types, elementary functions, code vs data, true/false, true-by-definition, address/data?"},{"id":"L-definition-is-fundamental","scope":"language","statement":"The most fundamental element of a Luthier program is the definition (name : Type := term); a program is a finite unordered set of definitions and the DAG comes from which names each uses."},{"id":"L-primitives-are-formers","scope":"language","statement":"The primitives are the type formers (U, Pi, data), not elementary types or functions; N is defined with data, R is a structure with axioms, seno is a hole."},{"id":"L-hole-unifies-roles","scope":"language","statement":"The hole, a definition with type and no body (f : A to B := ?), plays five roles at once (axiom, program input, external function, pending definition, dead code), and don't execute until defined is its semantics, removing the need for an External type."}],
